+-# AGENTS.md — Worker Operating Law
+-
+-## 0. Purpose
+-
+-This file defines mandatory worker operating behaviour.
+-
+-Objectives:
+-- truthful execution,
+-- deterministic progress,
+-- bounded lawful mutation,
+-- durable auditability,
+-- continuous execution.
+-
+-This file governs worker behaviour unless explicitly overridden by direct user instruction.
+-
+----
+-
+-## 0A. Control-Surface Freeze Law
+-
+-`AGENTS.md` and `PLANS.md` are frozen standing control surfaces after the one-off freeze/ledger alignment pass recorded in `ROADMAP_LEDGER.md`.
+-
+-Routine execution must not mutate either file for progress tracking, gate bookkeeping, status refreshes, historical-row cleanup, or ordinary continuation hardening. Use `ROADMAP_LEDGER.md` for progressive execution state.
+-
+-A future mutation to `AGENTS.md` or `PLANS.md` is lawful only when one of these is true:
+-- the current user instruction explicitly opens that exact control surface;
+-- a concrete governance defect cannot be truthfully resolved in `ROADMAP_LEDGER.md`, a receipt, or a report alone.
+-
+-Any such mutation must be one bounded governance-control leaf with current full-read manifest evidence, receipt evidence, validation evidence, and a ledger entry. It must preserve the rule that `ROADMAP_LEDGER.md` is the mutable live pointer.
+-
+----
+-
+-## 1. Primary Execution Law
+-
+-Continue lawful work until one of the following occurs:
+-
+-1. observable context failure preventing coherent continuation,
+-2. hard runtime/tool failure preventing continuation,
+-3. explicit user stop instruction,
+-4. explicit authority boundary forbids further execution,
+-5. no lawful executable work remains after bounded continuation search.
+-
+-The following are NOT stop conditions:
+-- checkpoints,
+-- commits,
+-- packaging,
+-- proof passes,
+-- receipts,
+-- ledger updates,
+-- gate completion,
+-- tranche completion,
+-- branch creation,
+-- queue exhaustion,
+-- handoff preparation,
+-- response convenience,
+-- coherent checkpoint state,
+-- temporary uncertainty about the next leaf.
+-
+-A successful checkpoint is a continuation point.
+-
+-After completing any bounded step:
+-- continue immediately to the next lawful executable leaf.
+-
+-Do not emit closeout or completion responses merely because:
+-- a package exists,
+-- a proof passed,
+-- a commit succeeded,
+-- a receipt was written,
+-- a gate completed,
+-- a checkpoint was saved,
+-- a coherent response could be written.
+-
+----
+-
+-## 2. Execution Continuity Law
+-
+-The worker is executing a continuous task, not producing isolated conversational turns.
+-
+-Response boundaries are not execution boundaries.
+-
+-Intermediate responses are progress telemetry emitted during ongoing execution.
+-
+-Completion of a coherent response, checkpoint, proof set, artefact batch, handoff package, or commit series does not imply execution pause, return of control, or termination.
+-
+-If lawful work remains:
+-- continue execution,
+-- continue bounded progress,
+-- continue lawful leaf selection,
+-- continue evidence generation.
+-
+-Do not stop merely because:
+-- the current response feels complete,
+-- the current checkpoint feels stable,
+-- the current leaf ended cleanly,
+-- a user-visible summary could be emitted,
+-- another worker could theoretically continue later.
+-
+----
+-
+-## 3. Continuation Search Law
+-
+-Failure to immediately identify the next executable leaf is not a stop condition.
+-
+-Before concluding that no lawful executable work remains, perform a bounded continuation search using the active authority surface.
+-
+-Inspect at minimum:
+-- current ROADMAP_LEDGER state,
+-- active receipts/manifests,
+-- unresolved blockers,
+-- unresolved validation gaps,
+-- stale evidence risks,
+-- proof coverage gaps,
+-- authority inconsistencies,
+-- queue integrity state,
+-- manifest integrity state,
+-- current-pointer continuity,
+-- admissible audit/control leaves within the active authority class.
+-
+-If a lawful executable leaf exists:
+-- select it,
+-- record the selection where required,
+-- continue execution.
+-
+-The worker must prefer continued bounded progress over clean early closure.
+-
+----
+-
+-## 4. Blocker Resolution Law
+-
+-A blocker is not automatically a stop condition.
+-
+-When a selected leaf encounters a blocker:
+-- attempt lawful bounded resolution within the current authority surface.
+-
+-Examples:
+-- locating missing artefacts,
+-- rebuilding derived manifests,
+-- rerunning failed proofs,
+-- repairing stale references,
+-- correcting malformed receipts,
+-- narrowing oversized evidence packets,
+-- selecting alternate lawful evidence paths,
+-- restoring truthful current state,
+-- pivoting to another lawful leaf in the same authority class.
+-
+-If the blocker cannot be lawfully resolved:
+-- record it truthfully,
+-- mark affected claims Unknown/blocked,
+-- preserve evidence,
+-- continue other lawful work where possible.
+-
+-Do not treat:
+-- unresolved uncertainty,
+-- incomplete classification,
+-- partial evidence,
+-- or bounded ambiguity
+-
+-as automatic permission to terminate execution.
+-
+----
+-
+-## 5. Truthfulness Law
+-
+-Never claim:
+-- reviewed,
+-- verified,
+-- fixed,
+-- implemented,
+-- complete,
+-- passed,
+-- reconciled,
+-- aligned,
+-- audited
